{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>Let it not be displeasing in the eyes of my lord that I cannot rise before you.</b> I do not understand what this excuse is — do women who have their way not rise and stand? Perhaps she said that her head and limbs were heavy upon her and she was ill at the onset of her way, for so is their nature, all the more so in the throes of childbirth, like Rachel, whose flux is scant and the way is very burdensome upon them. But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that in the days of the ancients, menstruants were kept at a great distance, for thus their name from of old has been \"menstruants\" (נִדּוֹת) for their distancing — they would not draw near to any person nor speak with him, for the ancients knew in their wisdom that their breath is harmful, and likewise their gaze produces blemish and makes an evil impression, as the philosophers explained; I am yet to mention their experiment in this matter (Leviticus 18:19). And they would sit alone in a tent into which no person would enter, as our Sages mentioned in the baraita of tractate Niddah: \"A student is forbidden to inquire after the welfare of a menstruant. Rabbi Nehemiah says: even the speech issuing from her mouth is impure.\" Rabbi Yohanan said: \"A person is forbidden to walk behind a menstruant and to step on her dust, for just as a corpse is impure, so is the dust of a menstruant impure,\" and it is forbidden to benefit from the work of her hands. Therefore Rachel said: I was worthy to rise before my lord and kiss his hands, but the way of women is upon me, and I cannot draw near to you, nor even walk about in the tent at all, lest you tread on the dust of my feet. And he fell silent before her and did not answer her, for they would not converse with them at all, because their speech is impure."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:55.784001940Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}